ACORN has played a vital role in the recent U.S. housing crisis, by creating a political movement to enact legislation which benefited poor people:

    "An obscure community-building group became a powerful organization with its first major victory in 1977 – a win for rent control in Washington, D.C. By 1980, with the organization’s support of union workers, a movement to raise the minimum wage became law. These early wins are at the heart of ACORN’s mission today – to give Americans a voice, and to make those voices heard."^{[1]}

ACORN helped draft and lobby the laws which led to the disastrous lending practices that led to thousands of people foreclosed on their homes.^{[2]} The media has been quick to condemn the group for its role in the foreclosures, while ignoring its activism for increased rent control and minimum wages.^{[3]}

